# Gate review, M-next D8 — the exporting state, prepare/encode, and the abort rule

**VERDICT: READY**, with two changes required in this slice before landing (findings 1 and 2). Neither is a safety defect: I found no path under which execution is wrongly restored — the dangerous failure the plan's risk 4 names ("a wrong early abort … creates two runners") cannot occur as written. Everything is conservative in the safe direction; the two required changes are a rule-coverage gap with a bounded liveness cost, and a missing test row for one contract limb whose implementation I verified by reading. All six attack points were checked against the landed code, not just the engineer's report.

What I read: the engineer's report; `git status` in the worktree (clean except the two new source files and the staged collab/ contracts — `handoff.cljc` untouched, as claimed); the r3 plan §§1.8/1.9 and the D8 test contract; astra's finding 9 and residual 2; the three rulings (D5 cursor, D5 close, D6 link-cursor); the new `export.cljc` and `export_test.cljc` in full; and the code they build on — `handoff.cljc` (`export-task`, `marker-for!`, `cell-at!`, `export-installs`, `call!`/`refuse!`), `remote.cljc` `lift-marker`, `completion.cljc` `complete`/`pull-parked`/`parked-slice`, `engine.cljc` (`gate-refuse!`, `handle-cursor-unminted`, `advance-install`, `check-wait-set`, `resume-continuation`, the applies), `vm.cljc` (`gate-mode`, `empty-state`), and `dao.stream`'s closed outcome sets plus `observe.cljc`'s classification table. No files edited, no suites run.

## Verified against the attack list

**1. `enter`.** The gate is set on the root (`export.cljc:154`), the wait set emptied (`:155`), and the reachable parked records moved by exactly the walk the lift runs: `reachable-parked` (`export.cljc:105-122`) reproduces `export-task`'s own walk arguments verbatim — `:cursor-profile (constantly :dao.stream.remote/v1)` and the same `module-decls` shape (`handoff.cljc:647-651`, `581-588`) — and the walk's `parked-slice` keys are the machine's own `:parked` pids (`completion.cljc:435-447`), i.e. exactly the records the root's lift encodes (`handoff.cljc:701-707`). `:yin.k/not-quiescent` fires on a non-empty ready queue in the root or any install child with the path and count (`export.cljc:91-98`), ahead of the portability refusals. All five ruling refusals fire — `:observe` entries (`:74-75`), `:yin.k/held` on any entry *or* cell (`:77-78`, covering both the D5 ruling's cell-held mint states and the D6 ruling's entry-held link states), unminted cursor cells (`:80-81`), pending closes (`:83-84`), cursorless `:link-request` entries (`:86-88`) — over the root and every install child (`machines`, `:51-59`), each reported with `:yin.k/hold` and `:yin.k/path`, and each fired on a real engine-parked machine in the test (`export_test.cljc:319-353`, child path `['foo]` asserted at `:351`). Children's waits and parked records deliberately stay inside the child machines; that is correct, because the blocker is the root's gate — `advance-install` does not run a child under `:exporting` (`engine.cljc:1396-1405`) — and the lift reaches children through `export-installs` recursion (`handoff.cljc:556-574`) over the reinstated root. `enter` refuses `:exporting`/`:ended` machines (`export.cljc:137-139`) and is pure on every refusal path.

**2. Prepare/encode.** Once-per-stream holds three ways: within one lift `marker-for!` memoizes per resource id in `found` (`handoff.cljc:159-174`) and `lift-marker` makes exactly one `serve!` call (`remote.cljc:144-157`); across root and children the wrapper's table is shared (`export.cljc:182-194`, one `export-task` per child gets the same `once`); across repeated prepares the atom is seeded from the record's `:served` — asserted by the test's re-prepare row (`export_test.cljc:377-379`). The `contains?`-keyed memo is load-bearing and correct: `lift-marker` treats a `nil` answer as a refusal, so a refused stream is remembered and not re-served within one prepare. Encode is pure as a matter of construction: the lifted machine's only effectful channel was `serve!` (astra finding 8's objection), and encode's `serve!` only reads the table (`export.cljc:204-211`); the fresh `found` atoms and byte building are in-memory. The miss-throw correctly escapes as a defect, not a refusal, because `call!` catches only throws whose ex-data carries `:yin.k/status` (`handoff.cljc:130-136`; the throw at `export.cljc:210` carries none) — matching the docstring and the test (`export_test.cljc:380-381`). Zero encode calls and equal bytes on repeat are asserted over two streams and over a real install-child machine (`:360-394`).

**3. The abort rule.** Against the replacement text: no attempts → `every?` over `[]` → legal ("before any possibly accepted offer attempt"); every attempt proving not-appended → legal; anything else refuses `:offer-possibly-accepted` (`export.cljc:254-255`). `:reply` is never consulted, so a refusal of one request is not evidence — pinned by the refusal-after-unknown row `[{} {:append :dao.stream/full :reply :yin.k/refused}]` and the ok-plus-refusal row (`export_test.cljc:445-449`). An attempt with no `:append` (unknown outcome, or intent durable but not yet sent) refuses — safe in both readings. The tenure gate requires `:yin.k/custody` ⇒ `{:now n :bound b}` with both present and `n < b` strictly (`export.cljc:248-251`): at and past the bound, and with no tenure evidence, `:tenure-ended` with the machine never restored (abort is pure); tenure does not license an uncertain offer (`export_test.cljc:469-472`). Both refusal orders are immaterial (either refuses). The rule's soundness additionally rests on the D14 write-ahead discipline (intent durable before send) — the docstring pins this as the precondition on `attempts`, which is the right seam. See finding 1 for the one substantive disagreement.

**4. Refusal completeness.** All five rulings' refusals are present and none extra is missing that I can find: FFI request/response entries travel as pending markers (`handoff.cljc:496-513`) and need no refusal; a machine not at a liftable safepoint (e.g. an active non-explicit-park record) is refused by the lift at prepare (`handoff.cljc:689-695`), which is the right split — `enter` owns the custody holds, prepare surfaces the lift's own refusals as data. The any-unminted-cell reading is assessed in finding 6.

**5. In-`:exporting` behavior.** Verified in the landed engine, then driven through `enter`'s machine: `check-wait-set` polls nothing under any gate and its docstring says so (`engine.cljc:2055-2090`); `advance-install` stamps and runs children in `:running` only; `resume-continuation` refuses through `gate-refuse!` *before* the parked lookup (`engine.cljc:2249-2259`, `479-487`); `apply-mint`, `apply-close`, `apply-observation` and `apply-link-cursor` all refuse in `:exporting`/`:ended` (`engine.cljc:571-594`, `698-706`, `1574-1610`, `1899`). The D8 test drives polling, a child tick, and a direct resume on the entered machine with zero counted calls (`export_test.cljc:264-298`). The applies are not driven through the entered machine — finding 3.

**6. Portability, style, deferrals.** Style matches the house pattern (ns docstring, ruled sections, per-fn docstrings, `:status`/`:yin.k/status` split mirroring handoff). Cross-host risks are contained: `reify` of the stream protocols is the established all-lane pattern (`engine_gate_test.cljc:32`), the `#?(:clj Exception :cljs js/Error :cljd Object)` catch matches it, bytes are compared via `vec` (the identity fix the engineer reported), and `export.cljc` itself has no host interop — keyword-set `contains?`, `apply dissoc` with a set tail, and map-keyed tables are portable. `empty-state` initializes `:parked {}`/`:wait-set []` (`vm.cljc:2221-2226`), so the unconditional assoc round-trip in `enter`/`abort` cannot synthesize spurious keys on real machines. Deferrals assessed in findings 4-6 and 8.

## Findings

1. **Required — `not-appended?`'s set is under-inclusive against the closed append outcome set.** `export.cljc:218-223` proves non-append only for `#{:dao.stream/full :dao.stream/closed}`, but `outcomes-append` is closed over `{ok full invalid-value closed refused transport-error}` (`dao/stream.cljc:90-96`). By the contract's own semantics, `refused` is "a policy answer … nothing was observed and nothing was recorded" and an effect's `invalid-value`/`closed` are terminal not-recorded answers (`dao/stream/observe.cljc:66-99`); `transport-error` is the one unknown-delivery outcome. So an offer attempt whose append answered `invalid-value` or `refused` is provably not appended, yet abort refuses. The direction is safe — it can only keep a fence up, never restore a second runner — and the wedge is bounded because the lease bound still ends the run (r3 1.6). But it is a real disagreement with the plan's permission ("every attempt's append answered an outcome that proves the value was not appended"), and a stable `refused`/`invalid-value` on the offer stream (policy refusal, oversize body) makes the abort path and the grant-to-self path both dead for that run. The docstring also enumerates a phantom "suspension" outcome (`:dao.stream/suspended` is front vocabulary, not an append outcome) while never addressing the two real ones it omits — the decision was made implicitly, not against the closed set. Fix in this slice: either widen to `#{full invalid-value closed refused}` with one justifying sentence citing `stream.cljc:90-96`, or keep the narrow set and write down that D11/D14's persisted attempt vocabulary will only ever record `ok`/`full`/`closed`/absent — otherwise the writer can persist an outcome the abort rule silently misclassifies. Two test rows either way.
2. **Required — the parked-record limb of `enter` has no test.** r3 1.9's sentence has three limbs; two are tested, the third — "moves … reachable parked records into the export record" — is not. No test machine holds a real reachable parked record (explicit park), so `(select-keys parked ids)` / `(apply dissoc parked ids)` (`export.cljc:147-160`) and `abort`'s merge-back (`:163-169`, `:258`) are never asserted, and the walk-refusal branch of `reachable-parked` (`:116-121`) is never exercised. The implementation is correct by reading — the id correspondence with the lift is exact (`completion.cljc:435-447` → `handoff.cljc:701-707`), and the round-trip types are sound (wait-sets are vectors end to end: `engine.cljc:2277-2281`; maps compare by content) — but the engineer's mutation evidence covers only `not-appended?`, so this limb has neither direct nor red evidence. Add one explicit-park row: after `enter`, the record holds the parked record, the machine's `:parked` lacks it, `prepare`/`encode` still lift it, and `abort` restores machine equality; and pin the intended asymmetry (children's parked records stay in the child) so a later refactor cannot silently change it.
3. **Low — the applies are not driven through `enter`'s machine.** The D4-D6 suite covers every apply under `:exporting` with a hand-assoc'd gate (`engine_gate_test.cljc:188-204`, `305-316`, `344-350`), and D8 asserts `(= :exporting (vm/gate-mode …))` on the entered machine, so the residual risk is only that `enter` stamps a key the applies don't read — which `vm/gate-mode` (`vm.cljc:600-604`) disproves. One cheap row (e.g. `apply-mint` refused on the entered machine) would close the "driven through these paths" loop the contract's phrasing asks for. Similarly, note that the "polling appends nothing" row is discharged by the emptied wait set (the entry was moved to the record) — correct by design, but it means a sweep-gate regression would be caught by the D4-D6 suite, not this one.
4. **Accepted deviation — `handoff.cljc`'s "prepare extracted" was not done.** The r3 D8 file list includes `handoff.cljc (prepare extracted)`. The engineer met the contract without it: prepare/encode live in `export.cljc` over the existing `export-task`, and the split's purity holds because the lift's sole impurity was `serve!`. Accept, with the note that the once-per-stream guarantee is now shouldered by `marker-for!`'s per-id memo *and* the wrapper's per-handle memo — the wrapper alone covers the caller-visible property and is the only cover for a handle shared across root and child machines. D9 rewrites this lift; the once-per-stream rows must survive that rewrite (they will — the suite carries).
5. **Correctly scoped, worth writing down — the served table is keyed by host handle.** `export.cljc:183-194` keys `:served` by the stream handle because that is all `lift-marker` hands `serve!` (`remote.cljc:144`). D14's recovery needs resource-id→descriptor from the stored prepared record (r3 1.10), and handles cannot be journaled — the engineer's concern 1 is accurate. The extraction belongs in D9 (which owns the lift and is next in `handoff.cljc`'s serialized order); record it as a D9/D14 acceptance note now so the answer-shape churn is expected, not discovered.
6. **Acceptable — the conservative any-unminted-cell/any-held-cell reading.** `refusal-at` scans all of `:resources` (`export.cljc:71-88`), not just walk-reachable cells; the rulings say "reachable". The over-refusal is transient and safe *given* D12's obligation to mint every unminted cell in `:seq` order (D5 ruling §2; r3 D12) — after the mint the cell carries `:yin.k/held` (still refused) until acknowledged, when `apply-mint` replaces it with a minted cell. Carry one caveat to D12: the driver must mint all unminted cells, including ones the program has dropped, or this refusal becomes permanent for such tasks.
7. **Note — vocabulary.** `:yin.k/refused` `:not-running` (re-enter), `:not-exporting`, `:offer-possibly-accepted`, `:tenure-ended`, the `{:now :bound}` tenure form, and the `:yin.k/hold` values are engineer-coined pending D13/D14 and the UCF 7.7.4/7.9/7.5.3 amendments — all honestly flagged in the report, and all already listed in r3 §5's amendment inventory. The 7.7.4/7.9 amendment text remains unwritten, as the brief allowed.
8. **Note — test hygiene.** Not test-first (implementation draft first, two real test fixes, one mutation for red evidence on the abort rule) — disclosed. The `counting-handle` and `throws?` helpers duplicate `engine_gate_test.cljc:25-49`; hoist to test-utils when a third consumer appears. JVM-only run is within the plan's cadence ("JVM per iteration; three lanes once at landing") but this suite is the gate's only evidence, so the three-lane run at landing is mandatory, not optional; kondo 0/0 is claimed, cljstyle left to the orchestrator as stated.

**Bottom line.** The slice implements r3 1.8/1.9 and all five export-refusal rulings faithfully, with the one real rule gap (finding 1) pointing in the safe direction and one contract limb awaiting its first test (finding 2). Land after those two are done; findings 3-8 are notes and correctly-scoped deferrals.
